<h2>Indian dried ginger for export</h2>
<p>India is a major global ginger producer, and the export trade runs predominantly on dried and processed forms — unbleached and bleached dried ginger, plus ground powder — rather than fresh rhizome. We source from Kerala and Karnataka, India's traditional ginger belt, along with significant volume from Meghalaya and Sikkim in the Northeast, where ginger is a major hill crop.</p>

<h2>Why dried, not fresh</h2>
<p>India's export policy treats fresh ginger differently from its dried and processed forms: fresh ginger carries an additional export-licensing restriction that dried, bleached and powdered ginger do not. In practice, this is one reason the dried product is the backbone of India's ginger export trade rather than fresh rhizome — it's both more shelf-stable for long-haul shipping and the more straightforwardly exportable form. If your programme specifically requires fresh ginger, talk to us about what's achievable; for most buyers, dried or powder form is the right specification regardless.</p>

<h2>A genuinely useful pricing insight: volume markets vs value markets</h2>
<p>Bangladesh buys roughly three-quarters of India's dried-ginger export volume but only about a third of its value — high-volume, moderate-unit-value bulk trade for food-service and retail use. Morocco, the United States, the UAE and European buyers, by contrast, pay materially more per kilogram for meaningfully smaller volumes — generally higher-grade, more processed or retail-packaged product. If you're benchmarking a quote against "what ginger costs," make sure you're comparing like-for-like: a Bangladesh bulk-volume price and a US/EU packaged-product price are not the same conversation.</p>

<h2>How should I specify dried ginger?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form.</strong> Unbleached, bleached, or ground powder — each serves different end uses and price points.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture.</strong> Standard export spec caps moisture at 10–12% for whole dried, 10% for powder — critical for shelf life and mould prevention.</li>
  <li><strong>Oil content.</strong> If your use case (flavouring, oleoresin extraction) is oil-content-sensitive, specify a target range — this varies by variety and processing.</li>
</ul>

